    "\$f'(x) = 3x^{2} - 2\$, so \$f''(x) = 6x\$. At \$x=1\$: \$f''(1) = 6(1) = 6\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3x^{2} - 3 = 3(x-1)(x+1)\$, so the critical points are \$x=-1\$ and \$x=1\$. For \$x\$ slightly less than \$-1\$, \$f'(x) > 0\$ (increasing); for \$x\$ slightly greater than \$-1\$, \$f'(x) < 0\$ (decreasing). Since \$f'\$ changes from positive to negative, \$x=-1\$ is a relative maximum.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3x^{2} - 6x = 3x(x-2)\$, so \$x=0\$ and \$x=2\$ are critical points. \$f''(x) = 6x - 6\$, so \$f''(2) = 6(2) - 6 = 6 > 0\$, meaning \$x=2\$ is a relative minimum by the second derivative test.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3x^{2}\$, so \$f'(2) = 12\$. With \$\\Delta x = -0.01\$: \$L(1.99) = f(2) + f'(2)(-0.01) = 8 - 0.12 = 7.88\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 4x^{3} - 6x\$. Differentiating again, \$f''(x) = 12x^{2} - 6\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 4x^{3} - 8x = 4x(x^{2}-2)\$, so the critical points are \$x=0, \\sqrt{2}, -\\sqrt{2}\$. \$f''(x) = 12x^{2} - 8\$, so \$f''(0) = -8 < 0\$. Since \$f''(0) < 0\$, the second derivative test says \$x=0\$ is a relative maximum.",
    "\$f'(x) = 6 x^{2} - 6 x - 12\$. Setting \$f'(x) = 0\$: \$6 x^{2} - 6 x - 12 = 0\$, which gives \$x = -1, 2\$.",
