    "Differentiate both sides of \$x^{2} + y^{2} = 25\$ implicitly with respect to \$x\$, treating \$y\$ as a function of \$x\$: \$2 x + 2 y(dy/dx) = 0\$. Solving for \$dy/dx\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{x}{y}\$. At \$(3,4)\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{3}{4} = - \\frac{3}{4}\$.",
    "Differentiate both sides of \$x^{3} + y^{3} = 9\$ implicitly with respect to \$x\$, treating \$y\$ as a function of \$x\$: \$3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}(dy/dx) = 0\$. Solving for \$dy/dx\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}\$. At \$(1,2)\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{1}{4} = - \\frac{1}{4}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[2 \\cdot x^{7}] = 2 \\cdot 7 x^{6} = 14 x^{6}\$; the derivative of the constant \$5\$ is \$0\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 14 x^{6}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[3 \\cdot \\sin(x)] = 3 \\cdot \\cos(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[3 \\cdot x^{4}] = 3 \\cdot 4 x^{3} = 12 x^{3}\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 12 x^{3}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[7 \\cdot x^{3}] = 7 \\cdot 3 x^{2} = 21 x^{2}\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[-2 \\cdot x^{2}] = -2 \\cdot 2 x^{1} = - 4 x\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{1}] = 1 x^{0} = 1\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 21 x^{2} - 4 x + 1\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\cos(x)] = -\\sin(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{2}] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 x^{1} = x\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[-3 \\cdot x^{1}] = -3 \\cdot 1 x^{0} = -3\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = x - 3\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin(x)] = \\cos(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin(x)] = \\cos(x)\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\cos(x)] = -\\sin(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}] = \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 1 + \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{5}] = 5 x^{4} = 5 x^{4}\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 5 x^{4}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{6}] = 6 x^{5} = 6 x^{5}\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[-4 \\cdot x^{1}] = -4 \\cdot 1 x^{0} = -4\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 6 x^{5} - 4\$.",
    "Differentiating \$x^2+y^2=C\$ implicitly: \$2x+2y\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0 \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\dfrac{0}{0}\$, so factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2}-16}{x-4} = \\dfrac{(x-4)(x+4)}{x-4} = x+4\$ for \$x \\neq 4\$. So \$\\lim_{x \\to 4}(x+4) = 8\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(x + 3\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 3\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2 + 3 = 5\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 1}{x - 1} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(x + 1\\right)}{x - 1}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 1\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 1\$). Substituting \$x=1\$: \$1 + 1 = 2\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 4}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 2\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2 + 2 = 4\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 5 x + 6}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(x - 2\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x - 3\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2 - 3 = -1\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 9}{x - 3} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(x + 3\\right)}{x - 3}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 3\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 3\$). Substituting \$x=3\$: \$3 + 3 = 6\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{3} - 8}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(x^{2} + 2 x + 4\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x^{2} + 2 x + 4\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2^{2} + 2 \\cdot 2 + 4 = 12\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{1}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{3 x + 1}{6 x + 5} = \\dfrac{3 + \\frac{1}{x}}{6 + \\frac{5}{x}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{3 x + 1}{6 x + 5} = \\frac{1}{2}\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{2}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x + 3}{x^{2} - 4} = \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{3}{x^{2}}}{1 - \\frac{4}{x^{2}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{x + 3}{x^{2} - 4} = 0\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{2}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x \\left(2 x + 3\\right)}{5 x^{2} - x + 1} = \\dfrac{2 + \\frac{3}{x}}{5 - \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^{2}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{2 x^{2} + 3 x}{5 x^{2} - x + 1} = \\frac{2}{5}\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{3}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x \\left(4 x^{2} - 1\\right)}{2 x^{3} + 7} = \\dfrac{4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}}{2 + \\frac{7}{x^{3}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{4 x^{3} - x}{2 x^{3} + 7} = 2\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{3}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} + 1}{x^{3} + 2} = \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}}}{1 + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{x^{2} + 1}{x^{3} + 2} = 0\$.",
    "Dividing numerator and denominator by \$x\$: \$\\dfrac{3 + 1/x}{1 - 5/x} \\to \\dfrac{3}{1} = 3\$ as \$x \\to \\infty\$. So \$y=3\$ is the horizontal asymptote.",
