    "If x and y are both given as functions of a parameter t, the chain-rule formula for dy/dx in terms of t is:",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int 2 \\cdot x^{1} \\, dx = 2 \\cdot \\dfrac{x^{2}}{2}\$; \$\\int 7 \\, dx = 7x\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = x^{2} + 7 x + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int 3 \\cdot \\sin(x) \\, dx = 3 \\cdot -\\cos(x)\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\, dx = 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{x^{3}}{3}\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = x^{3} + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int 4 \\cdot x^{3} \\, dx = 4 \\cdot \\dfrac{x^{4}}{4}\$; \$\\int -2 \\cdot x^{1} \\, dx = -2 \\cdot \\dfrac{x^{2}}{2}\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = x^{4} - x^{2} + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int 6 \\cdot x^{5} \\, dx = 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{x^{6}}{6}\$; \$\\int -3 \\, dx = -3x\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = x^{6} - 3 x + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int \\cos(x) \\, dx = \\sin(x)\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int \\sec^2(x) \\, dx = \\tan(x)\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = \\frac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}} + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int \\sin(x) \\, dx = -\\cos(x)\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + C\$.",
    "Integrate term by term using the power rule for antiderivatives (\$\\int x^n \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\$) and the standard trigonometric antiderivatives: \$\\int x^{4} \\, dx = \\dfrac{x^{5}}{5}\$; \$\\int 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\, dx = 5 \\cdot \\dfrac{x^{3}}{3}\$. Summing the terms and adding the constant of integration: \$\\int f(x) \\, dx = \\frac{x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{5 x^{3}}{3} + C\$.",
    "Just below \$3\$, on the interval \$[2, 3)\$, the greatest integer function \$[x]\$ stays constant at \$2\$ — it only steps up to \$3\$ once \$x\$ reaches \$3\$ itself. So the left-hand limit is \$\\lim_{x \\to 3^-} [x] = 2\$.",
