    "Reflecting about the x-axis multiplies the function by \$-1\$, giving \$-\\sqrt{x}\$. Shifting 1 unit down then subtracts 1: \$g(x) = -\\sqrt{x} - 1\$.",
    "Replace \$x\$ by \$-x\$ and \$y\$ by \$-y\$: \$(-x)(-y) = xy\$, which equals the original left-hand side. Since the equation \$xy=4\$ is unchanged under this replacement, the curve is symmetric about the origin.",
    "Replace \$x\$ by \$-x\$: \$(-x)^{3} - (-x) = -x^{3} + x = -(x^{3}-x)\$. Since this equals the negative of the original expression, i.e. \$f(-x) = -f(x)\$, the function is odd and its graph is symmetric about the origin.",
    "Replace \$x\$ by \$-x\$: \$(-x)^{4} - 3(-x)^{2} + 1 = x^{4} - 3x^{2} + 1\$, which is identical to the original equation. Since the equation is unchanged when \$x\$ is replaced by \$-x\$, the curve is symmetric about the y-axis.",
    "Replacing \$x\$ with \$cx\$ (for \$c>1\$) inside a function compresses its graph horizontally by a factor of \$c\$. Here \$y=(2x)^{3}\$ replaces \$x\$ with \$2x\$, so the graph of \$y=x^{3}\$ is compressed horizontally by a factor of \$2\$.",
    "Rewrite \$\\dfrac{\\sin(3x)}{5x}\$ as \$\\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin(3x)}{3x}\$. Using \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(kx)}{kx} = 1\$ with \$k=3\$, the second factor tends to \$1\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(3x)}{5x} = \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{3}{5}\$.",
    "Rewrite \$\\dfrac{\\sin(4x)}{x}\$ as \$4 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin(4x)}{4x}\$. Using \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(kx)}{kx} = 1\$ with \$k=4\$, the second factor tends to \$1\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(4x)}{x} = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4\$.",
    "Rolle's Theorem requires a function f to be continuous on [a,b], differentiable on (a,b), and f(a) = f(b). Under these conditions, the theorem guarantees:",
    "Rolle's Theorem states that if \$f(a) = f(b)\$ and \$f\$ is continuous on \$[a,b]\$ and differentiable on \$(a,b)\$, then there exists at least one \$c \\in (a,b)\$ with \$f'(c) = 0\$ -- the tangent is horizontal somewhere between the two equal-height points.",
    "Set \$x=0\$: \$0 + y^{2} = 9\$, so \$y^{2} = 9\$, giving \$y = 3\$ or \$y = -3\$.",
    "Set \$y = 2x+3\$ and solve for \$x\$: \$y - 3 = 2x\$, so \$x = \\dfrac{y-3}{2}\$. Swapping the roles of \$x\$ and \$y\$ gives \$f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x-3}{2}\$.",
    "Set \$y = 5x-2\$ and solve for \$x\$: \$x = \\dfrac{y+2}{5}\$, so \$f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{5}\$. Then \$f^{-1}(8) = \\dfrac{8+2}{5} = \\dfrac{10}{5} = 2\$. (Check: \$f(2) = 5(2)-2 = 8\$, confirming the inverse.)",
    "Set \$y = \\dfrac{x+1}{2}\$ and solve for \$x\$: \$2y = x+1\$, so \$x = 2y - 1\$. Swapping \$x\$ and \$y\$ gives \$f^{-1}(x) = 2x - 1\$.",
    "Set \$y = x^{3}\$ and solve for \$x\$: taking the cube root of both sides, \$x = y^{1/3}\$. Swapping \$x\$ and \$y\$ gives \$f^{-1}(x) = x^{1/3}\$.",
    "Shifting a graph \$c\$ units to the right replaces \$x\$ with \$x-c\$ inside the function, and shifting it \$d\$ units up adds \$d\$ to the whole function. So \$f(x) = x^{2}\$ shifted 3 right and 2 up becomes \$g(x) = (x-3)^{2} + 2\$.",
    "Simplify: \$\\frac{x^{2} - 25}{x - 5} = x + 5\$ for \$x \\neq 5\$, so \$\\lim_{x \\to 5} f(x) = 5 + 5 = 10\$, but the given \$f(5) = 7 \\neq 10\$. The limit exists but does not equal \$f(5)\$, so \$f\$ is discontinuous at \$x=5\$.",
    "Simplify: \$\\frac{x^{2} - 25}{x - 5} = x + 5\$ for \$x \\neq 5\$, so \$\\lim_{x \\to 5} f(x) = 5 + 5 = 10\$. This equals the given \$f(5) = 10\$, and since the limit exists and matches \$f(5)\$, \$f\$ is continuous at \$x=5\$.",
    "Since \$\\cosh(x) = \\dfrac{e^{x}+e^{-x}}{2}\$, differentiating gives \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\cosh(x)] = \\dfrac{e^{x}-e^{-x}}{2} = \\sinh(x)\$.",
    "Since \$\\sinh(x) = \\dfrac{e^{x}-e^{-x}}{2}\$, differentiating gives \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sinh(x)] = \\dfrac{e^{x}+e^{-x}}{2} = \\cosh(x)\$.",
    "Since \$b=0=c^2\$ with \$c=0\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-0=(x-(0))(x+0)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-0}{x-(0)}=x+0\$ for \$x\\neq 0\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to 0}(x+0)=0+0=0\$.",
    "Since \$b=16=c^2\$ with \$c=4\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-16=(x-(4))(x+4)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-16}{x-(4)}=x+4\$ for \$x\\neq 4\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to 4}(x+4)=4+4=8\$.",
    "Since \$b=1=c^2\$ with \$c=-1\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-1=(x-(-1))(x- 1)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-(-1)}=x- 1\$ for \$x\\neq -1\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to -1}(x- 1)=-1- 1=-2\$.",
    "Since \$b=1=c^2\$ with \$c=1\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-1=(x-(1))(x+1)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-(1)}=x+1\$ for \$x\\neq 1\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to 1}(x+1)=1+1=2\$.",
    "Since \$b=25=c^2\$ with \$c=-5\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-25=(x-(-5))(x- 5)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-25}{x-(-5)}=x- 5\$ for \$x\\neq -5\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to -5}(x- 5)=-5- 5=-10\$.",
    "Since \$b=25=c^2\$ with \$c=5\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-25=(x-(5))(x+5)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-25}{x-(5)}=x+5\$ for \$x\\neq 5\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to 5}(x+5)=5+5=10\$.",
    "Since \$b=36=c^2\$ with \$c=6\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-36=(x-(6))(x+6)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-36}{x-(6)}=x+6\$ for \$x\\neq 6\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to 6}(x+6)=6+6=12\$.",
    "Since \$b=4=c^2\$ with \$c=-2\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-4=(x-(-2))(x- 2)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-(-2)}=x- 2\$ for \$x\\neq -2\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to -2}(x- 2)=-2- 2=-4\$.",
    "Since \$b=4=c^2\$ with \$c=2\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-4=(x-(2))(x+2)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-(2)}=x+2\$ for \$x\\neq 2\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to 2}(x+2)=2+2=4\$.",
    "Since \$b=9=c^2\$ with \$c=-3\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-9=(x-(-3))(x- 3)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x-(-3)}=x- 3\$ for \$x\\neq -3\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to -3}(x- 3)=-3- 3=-6\$.",
    "Since \$b=9=c^2\$ with \$c=3\$, the numerator factors: \$x^2-9=(x-(3))(x+3)\$. So \$\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x-(3)}=x+3\$ for \$x\\neq 3\$, and \$\\lim_{x\\to 3}(x+3)=3+3=6\$.",
    "Since \$f(x) = 3 x^{2} - 5 x + 7\$ is continuous at \$x=2\$ (the denominator, if any, is not zero there), substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x \\to 2} 3 x^{2} - 5 x + 7 = 3 \\cdot 2^{2} - 5 \\cdot 2 + 7 = 9\$.",
    "Since \$f(x) = 5 x - 7\$ is continuous at \$x=3\$ (the denominator, if any, is not zero there), substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x \\to 3} 5 x - 7 = 5 \\cdot 3 - 7 = 8\$.",
    "Since \$f(x) = \\frac{2 x + 3}{x - 1}\$ is continuous at \$x=4\$ (the denominator, if any, is not zero there), substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x \\to 4} \\frac{2 x + 3}{x - 1} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 + 3}{4 - 1} = \\frac{11}{3}\$.",
    "Since \$f(x) = \\frac{x^{2} + 2 x}{x + 5}\$ is continuous at \$x=-2\$ (the denominator, if any, is not zero there), substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x \\to -2} \\frac{x^{2} + 2 x}{x + 5} = \\frac{\\left(-2\\right) \\left(2 + -2\\right)}{-2 + 5} = 0\$.",
    "Since \$f(x) = \\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x + 3}\$ is continuous at \$x=1\$ (the denominator, if any, is not zero there), substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x + 3} = \\frac{1^{2} - 1}{1 + 3} = 0\$.",
    "Since \$f(x) = x^{3} - 4 x + 1\$ is continuous at \$x=-1\$ (the denominator, if any, is not zero there), substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x \\to -1} x^{3} - 4 x + 1 = \\left(-1\\right)^{3} - 4 \\left(-1\\right) + 1 = 4\$.",
    "Since \$f(x)=2x+1\$ is continuous everywhere, substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2x+1) = 2\\cdot(0)+1 = 1\$.",
    "Since \$f(x)=2x+4\$ is continuous everywhere, substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2x+4) = 2\\cdot(0)+4 = 4\$.",
