    "Solve \$5x- 3=1\$ for \$x\$: \$x=\\dfrac{1-(-3)}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}\$.",
    "Standard limit: \$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=0\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to -2}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(-2)^2-2(-2)+1 = 17\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to -3}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(-3)^2-2(-3)+1 = 34\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to -4}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(-4)^2-2(-4)+1 = 57\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 0}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(0)^2-2(0)+1 = 1\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 1}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(1)^2-2(1)+1 = 2\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(2)^2-2(2)+1 = 9\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 3}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(3)^2-2(3)+1 = 22\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 4}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(4)^2-2(4)+1 = 41\$.",
    "Substitute directly: \$\\lim_{x\\to 5}(3x^2-2x+1) = 3(5)^2-2(5)+1 = 66\$.",
    "Substituting the exponential definitions and expanding confirms \$\\cosh^{2}(x) - \\sinh^{2}(x) = 1\$ for all \$x\$ -- the hyperbolic analogue of \$\\cos^{2}\\theta + \\sin^{2}\\theta = 1\$.",
    "The Mean Value Theorem guarantees a point \$c\$ where the instantaneous rate of change \$f'(c)\$ equals the average rate of change over the whole interval, \$\\dfrac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}\$ -- geometrically, the tangent at \$c\$ is parallel to the secant line through \$(a,f(a))\$ and \$(b,f(b))\$.",
    "The Mean Value Theorem states that for f continuous on [a,b] and differentiable on (a,b), there exists c in (a,b) such that f'(c) equals:",
    "The arc length formula for a smooth curve \$y = f(x)\$ from x = a to x = b is:",
    "The arc length of \$y=f(x)\$ from \$x=a\$ to \$x=b\$ is \$L = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^{2}} \\, dx\$, derived from summing tiny hypotenuse lengths \$\\sqrt{(dx)^2+(dy)^2}\$ along the curve.",
    "The change-of-base formula expresses log_b(x) (log base b of x) in terms of natural logarithms as:",
    "The change-of-base formula is \$\\log_b(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln b}\$, which lets any logarithm be computed or differentiated using natural logarithms.",
    "The denominator vanishes at \$x = 3\$ (and the numerator, \$x+1=4\$, does not), so \$x = 3\$ is a vertical asymptote.",
    "The differential dy of a function \$y = f(x)\$ is defined as:",
    "The differential is defined as \$dy = f'(x)\\,dx\$ -- it approximates (but is not exactly equal to) the true change \$\\Delta y = f(x+dx) - f(x)\$ for small \$dx\$, using the tangent line's slope.",
    "The displacement of a particle moving along a line is s(t) = t^3 - 6t^2 + 9t. Find its velocity at t = 2.",
    "The domain of \$f+g\$ is the intersection of the domains of \$f\$ and \$g\$. \$f(x) = \\sqrt{x}\$ requires \$x \\geq 0\$, while \$g(x) = x - 4\$ is defined for all real \$x\$. The intersection is \$x \\geq 0\$.",
    "The exact area under \$y = x^2\$ on [0,2], found by evaluating the definite integral directly, is:",
    "The function \$f(x) = (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2)\$ is undefined at x = 2. Classify the discontinuity at x = 2.",
    "The function \$f(x) = x^2\$ for x >= 0 has inverse \$f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{x}\$. Find \$f^{-1}(9)\$.",
    "The fundamental hyperbolic identity relating cosh(x) and sinh(x) is:",
    "The graph of \$y = f(x)\$ is stretched vertically by a factor of 3 and shifted 2 units to the left. Which formula describes the new graph in terms of f?",
    "The graph of \$y = x^2\$ is shifted 4 units to the right and 8 units down to form \$y = (x-4)^2 - 8\$. What is the lowest point (vertex) of the new graph?",
    "The greatest integer function \$f(x) = [x] (\$the largest integer <= x) is discontinuous at every integer. Classify the discontinuity at x = 2.",
    "The half-life is the time \$T\$ for which \$y(T) = \\dfrac{y_0}{2}\$, i.e. \$e^{-kT} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\$, so \$kT = \\ln 2\$, giving \$T = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\$. With \$k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{30}\$: \$T = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 2 / 30} = 30\$ years.",
    "The hyperbolic sine function, sinh(x), is defined in terms of the exponential function as:",
    "The slope is \$f'(-1)\$ where \$f'(x) = 4 x\$, so \$f'(-1) = 4 \\left(-1\\right) = -4\$.",
    "The slope is \$f'(1)\$ where \$f'(x) = 2 x - 4\$, so \$f'(1) = 2 \\cdot 1 - 4 = -2\$.",
    "The slope is \$f'(2)\$ where \$f'(x) = 3 x^{2} - 3\$, so \$f'(2) = 3 \\cdot 2^{2} - 3 = 9\$.",
    "The slope is \$f'(3)\$ where \$f'(x) = 2 x\$, so \$f'(3) = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6\$.",
    "The slope of the tangent at \$x=x_0\$ is \$y'(x_0)\$. Here \$y' = 3x^{2}\$, so at \$x_0=1\$, the slope is \$y'(1) = 3(1)^{2} = 3\$.",
    "The tangent line to \$y = \\sqrt{x}\$ at x = 4 is used to estimate sqrt(4.1). What estimate does it give?",
    "The tangent line to \$y = x^3\$ at x = 2 is used to estimate (1.99)^3. What estimate does it give?",
    "The velocity of a particle is v(t) = 3t^2 - 6t. Find its acceleration at t = 2.",
    "The vertex of \$y=x^{2}\$ is at the origin \$(0,0)\$. Shifting 4 units right moves the x-coordinate to \$4\$, and shifting 8 units down moves the y-coordinate to \$-8\$, giving the new vertex \$(4,-8)\$.",
    "The x-intercepts satisfy \$y=0\$: \$x^{2} - 5x + 6 = 0\$, which factors as \$(x-2)(x-3) = 0\$, giving \$x=2\$ or \$x=3\$.",
    "This is a \$0/0\$ form. Differentiate numerator and denominator separately: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[1 - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}] = \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\$ and \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{2}] = 2 x\$. By L'Hopital's Rule, \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{1 - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{2}} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{2 x} = \\frac{\\sin{\\left(0 \\right)}}{2 \\cdot 0} = \\frac{1}{2}\$.",
    "This is a \$0/0\$ form. Differentiate numerator and denominator separately: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}] = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$ and \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x] = 1\$. By L'Hopital's Rule, \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{x} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{1} = \\cos{\\left(0 \\right)} = 1\$.",
    "This is a \$0/0\$ form. Differentiate numerator and denominator separately: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{2} - 1] = 2 x\$ and \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x - 1] = 1\$. By L'Hopital's Rule, \$\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x - 1} = \\lim_{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{2 x}{1} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\$.",
    "This is a \$0/0\$ form. Differentiate numerator and denominator separately: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{2} - 4] = 2 x\$ and \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x - 2] = 1\$. By L'Hopital's Rule, \$\\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{x^{2} - 4}{x - 2} = \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{2 x}{1} = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\$.",
    "This is a polynomial, so it's continuous everywhere and the limit equals direct substitution: \$4(-2)^{2} - 3(-2) + 1 = 16+6+1 = 23\$.",
    "This is the definition of the definite integral: \$\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^{*}) \\Delta x\$, the limit of Riemann sums as the rectangles become infinitely thin.",
    "To find \$f^{-1}(11)\$, solve \$f(x) = 11\$ for \$x\$: \$5 x + 1 = 11\$. Rearranging gives \$x = 2\$, so \$f^{-1}(11) = 2\$.",
    "To find \$f^{-1}(2)\$, solve \$f(x) = 2\$ for \$x\$: \$6 - x = 2\$. Rearranging gives \$x = 4\$, so \$f^{-1}(2) = 4\$.",
    "To find \$f^{-1}(3)\$, solve \$f(x) = 3\$ for \$x\$: \$2 x + 7 = 3\$. Rearranging gives \$x = -2\$, so \$f^{-1}(3) = -2\$.",
    "To find \$f^{-1}(5)\$, solve \$f(x) = 5\$ for \$x\$: \$3 x - 4 = 5\$. Rearranging gives \$x = 3\$, so \$f^{-1}(5) = 3\$.",
    "To find \$f^{-1}(9)\$, solve \$f(x) = 9\$ for \$x\$: \$4 x - 3 = 9\$. Rearranging gives \$x = 3\$, so \$f^{-1}(9) = 3\$.",
    "To obtain the graph of \$y = (2x)^3\$ from the graph of \$y = x^3\$, you should:",
    "Using an appropriate substitution for the inner expression, \$\\int \\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\, dx = - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{5} + C\$.",
    "Using integration by parts with \$u = x\$, \$dv = e^{x} \\, dx\$: \$v = e^{x}\$, so \$\\int x \\, e^{x} \\, dx = xe^{x} - \\int e^{x} \\, dx = \\left(x - 1\\right) e^{x} + C\$. Evaluating from \$0\$ to \$1\$: \$(\\left(-1 + 1\\right) e^{1}) - (\\left(-1 + 0\\right) e^{0}) = 0 - -1 = 1\$.",
    "Using substitution, an antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{\\log{\\left(x^{2} + 4 \\right)}}{2}\$. Evaluating from \$x=0\$ to \$x=2\$: \$F(2) - F(0) = \\log{\\left(2 \\sqrt{2} \\right)} - \\log{\\left(2 \\right)} = \\log{\\left(\\sqrt{2} \\right)}\$.",
    "Using substitution, an antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{\\sin^{3}{\\left(x \\right)}}{3}\$. Evaluating from \$x=0\$ to \$x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\$: \$F(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) - F(0) = \\frac{1}{3} - 0 = \\frac{1}{3}\$.",
    "Using substitution, an antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{x^{8}}{4} + x^{6} + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + x^{2}\$. Evaluating from \$x=0\$ to \$x=1\$: \$F(1) - F(0) = \\frac{15}{4} - 0 = \\frac{15}{4}\$.",
    "Using the change-of-base identity \$\\log_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln 2}\$: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln 2}\\right] = \\dfrac{1}{\\ln 2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x} = \\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 2}\$.",
    "Using the quotient rule on \$\\tanh(x) = \\dfrac{\\sinh(x)}{\\cosh(x)}\$: \$\\tanh'(x) = \\dfrac{\\cosh^{2}(x) - \\sinh^{2}(x)}{\\cosh^{2}(x)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh^{2}(x)}\$, which (using \$\\cosh^{2}(x) = 1 + \\sinh^{2}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-\\tanh^{2}(x)}\$) simplifies to \$1 - \\tanh^{2}(x)\$.",
    "Using the standard derivative formula for inverse trigonometric functions: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\arccos(x)] = - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}\$.",
    "Using the standard derivative formula for inverse trigonometric functions: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\arcsin(x)] = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}\$.",
    "Using the standard derivative formula for inverse trigonometric functions: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\arctan(x)] = \\frac{1}{x^{2} + 1}\$.",
    "Using the standard limit \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\cos(x) - 1}{x} = 0\$ (equivalently \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(x)}{x} = 0\$, since \$1-\\cos x\$ is a sign flip of \$\\cos x - 1\$): \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{1 - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{x} = 0\$.",
    "Velocity is \$v(t) = s'(t) = 16 - 2t\$. The ball is at its highest point when \$v(t)=0\$: \$16 - 2t = 0 \\Rightarrow t = 8\$.",
    "Velocity is \$v(t) = s'(t) = 3t^{2} - 12t + 9\$. At \$t=2\$: \$v(2) = 3(2)^{2} - 12(2) + 9 = 12 - 24 + 9 = -3\$.",
    "Vertical asymptotes of a proper rational function occur where the denominator is zero (and the numerator is not): \$x^{2} - 9 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 3\$ or \$x=-3\$.",
    "What is the domain of \$f(x) = 1/(x-3)?\$",
    "What is the domain of \$f(x) = 1/(x^2-4)?\$",
    "What is the domain of \$f(x) = 1/\\sqrt{x+5}?\$",
    "What is the domain of \$f(x) = \\sqrt{9-x^2}?\$",
    "What is the domain of \$f(x) = \\sqrt{x+1}/(x-4)?\$",
    "What is the domain of \$f(x) = \\sqrt{x-2}?\$",
    "With \$2(x+y)=20\$, area \$A=xy=x\\left(\\dfrac{20}{2}-x\\right)\$ is maximised when \$x=y=\\dfrac{20}{4}\$ (a square), giving \$A=\\left(\\dfrac{20}{4}\\right)^2=25\$.",
    "With \$2(x+y)=24\$, area \$A=xy=x\\left(\\dfrac{24}{2}-x\\right)\$ is maximised when \$x=y=\\dfrac{24}{4}\$ (a square), giving \$A=\\left(\\dfrac{24}{4}\\right)^2=36\$.",
